More resilient, adaptable and trustworthy AI in real settings, with less re-engineering and higher uptime for operators; no numeric targets are set.
The call expects AI that:
- sustains performance in changing conditions;
- needs fewer costly re-engineering and retraining cycles;
- adapts quickly to new products, services, regulations and usage patterns;
- supports personalised services without compromising safety;
- improves resilience and uptime for industry and public sector operators;
- is more transparent and controllable over time;
- is easier to deploy in safety-critical or high-liability contexts.
It also aims to build a European community in trustworthy AI and support the AI Act.

TenneT would be most credible as owner of a safety-critical grid-control use case, with a simulated grid setting and operator expertise.
This is an interpretation. The call accepts digital twins and safety-critical systems as domains and requires validation in a lab or simulated setting with long-horizon drift. TenneT could offer:
- a grid-operation use case, for example AI agents that suggest topology actions, building on GNN4GC (graph neural networks for grid control);
- a simulated environment with realistic drift, such as changing renewable feed-in and grid topology, through the digital twin facility it runs with Delft University of Technology (NFDTES);
- control-room knowledge on human oversight, escalation and auditability requirements.
Since projects need at least two domains, TenneT would usually cover one domain in a consortium led by an AI research group.
